# Instructions

## Project Files

- `index.html` contains the page markup and links to the stylesheet and script.
- `styles.css` contains all visual styling and responsive rules.
- `script.js` contains the lead form submit-button behaviour and custom enquiry dropdown interactions.

## Landing Page Format

This version is structured as a focused lead-generation landing page:

1. Hero section with the primary offer and operating snapshot.
2. Trust strip with high-value proof numbers.
3. Service section explaining what AMH manages.
4. Process/platform section explaining how the engagement works.
5. Proof section for credibility.
6. Partner strip.
7. Final CTA followed immediately by the lead form.
8. Original-style footer with readable contact, service and credential columns.

## What Was Added

- A single primary conversion form below the final CTA.
- Clearer section headers and shorter, more readable paragraphs.
- A trust strip immediately after the hero.
- A process section to explain what happens after enquiry.
- A final CTA section that sends users back to the form.
- Larger body text, stronger contrast and more comfortable spacing.
- Mobile-first refinements for narrow phones, tablets and desktop.
- A compact landing-page header with only brand and the primary CTA.
- A restored multi-column footer based on the original design, with better readability.

## What Was Removed Or Reduced

- The duplicate hero mini-form and separate contact form pattern.
- Repeated stats sections.
- Distracting footer link columns and external promotional links.
- Very small text styles that made the page harder to read.
- Website-style header navigation and hamburger menu.
- Extra vertical padding that made sections feel too tall to scan.
- Header credential text and footer certification badges.

## GetResponse Implementation Notes

STEP 1: In GetResponse, go to Convert > Landing Pages > Create landing page.
STEP 2: Choose the blank/empty template.
STEP 3: Open the HTML or Source editor.
STEP 4: Paste the contents of `index.html`.
STEP 5: Make sure `styles.css` and `script.js` are hosted somewhere GetResponse can load, then update these lines in `index.html` if needed:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
<script src="script.js"></script>
```

If GetResponse does not allow separate files, paste the contents of `styles.css` inside a `<style>` block in the `<head>`, and paste the contents of `script.js` inside a `<script>` block before `</body>`.

### Current plain-HTML form wiring (already applied in `index.html`)

- Form action: `https://app.getresponse.com/add_subscriber.html`
- Required hidden inputs:
  - `campaign_token` (replace placeholder with your real list token)
  - `thankyou_url` (set your redirect URL, or leave empty for default behavior)
  - `start_day` (`0`)
- Standard fields:
  - `email`
  - `name`
- Custom fields follow GetResponse format:
  - `custom_company`
  - `custom_phone`
  - `custom_enquiry_type`
  - `custom_message`

Important:
- In your GetResponse list settings, enable plain HTML form signups (blocked by default on many new lists).
- If you are on MAX, use your sending domain endpoint instead of `app.getresponse.com`.

## Before Publishing

A) CONTACT FORM: Replace the lead form block below the final CTA with your GetResponse form embed code.
Go to Forms and Popups > Create Form > Embed, then paste the embed code in the marked form area.

B) FORM BEHAVIOUR: The current placeholder form uses `mailto:`. For a live landing page, GetResponse should handle the actual lead capture.

C) HEADER: The header is intentionally minimal for landing-page conversion. It contains no hamburger menu, no multi-link website navigation and no credential text.

D) QR CODE: The old QR note was preserved from the original version, but there is currently no QR image placeholder in the page. Add one only if the campaign needs it.

E) FONTS: Google Fonts loads through the existing `<link>` tag in `index.html`.
